ready: 1
kind: module
id: memory
name: Memory
summary: >-
  Owns the stratified memory records of a stack: writing, updating, and revising every kind,
  their versions and provenance links, the content-free change ledger, listing, lineage, and
  dump. Runs in the Kernle core inside the daemon, or in-process when the daemon is stopped.
runs_in:
  - sys.daemon
  - sys.python
  - sys.cli
owns:
  - memory (DATA-MEMORY)
  - memory versions
  - provenance links
  - change ledger
invariants:
  - Every memory of every kind is written through one write path.
  - A memory, its version, its links, and its change row commit together or not at all.
  - Every memory can be traced to the captures, events, and memories it came from.
  - The change ledger never holds memory content.
rationale: >-
  One table holds every memory kind, with the fields that differ by kind in a validated payload.
  Decay, trust, recall ranking, provenance, deletion, and sync then work the same way for every
  kind, which is what keeps the layers consistent. The kinds themselves keep their own calls so
  agents write an episode, a belief, or a value in the words they already use.


  The change ledger is the single signal that something changed. The search index and the sync
  queue both read it after their own cursors, so no writer can forget to update either one.
